WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.779
Although IT: Chapter One did a wonderful job
of capturing the characters and the feel of

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.580
the book, a lot of the novel's specifics were
significantly altered.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:13.280
Now that the story is complete with the release
of IT: Chapter Two, we have a more complete

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.800
picture of how the films match up to the book.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.700
Both the book and the movie jump back and
forth between two different time periods nearly

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:27.160
three decades apart, but while the book alternates
between 1957 and 1985, the films are set in

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.960
1989 and 2016.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.900
Considering the novel was originally released
in 1986, making the '80s timeline feel tangibly

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:39.260
recent to its readers, it makes sense that
director Andy Muschietti and his team would

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.920
update the film to feel contemporary to today's
audiences.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.100
Although much of Derry and the story of IT
exists in what feels like a sort of timeless

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.820
bubble, making the decade almost irrelevant,
the modern-day setting does alter the story

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.940
in a few minor but effective ways.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Although both the book and the films focus
on the same group of characters in two different

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:05.150
time periods, the films draw a much firmer
line between past and present, with none of

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:09.220
the adult versions of the characters even
making an appearance in IT: Chapter One.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.940
While Chapter Two isn't quite as distinct
in its focus and includes a fair number of

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.740
flashbacks, it's still accurate to say that
Chapter One is about the kids and Chapter

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.160
Two is about the adults.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.520
This is very different from King's book, which
braids the two time periods together.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:29.480
Streamlining King's narrative likely worked
to the films' benefit, since it may have felt

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.520
jarring to visually jump between time periods
as often as the book does.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.360
Splitting the films into a children-centric
film and an adult-centric film allowed each

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:43.579
one to end with a climactic showdown against
Pennywise, even if the kids' victory would

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.680
prove tragically impermanent.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.440
When we first meet the adult Losers, they
haven't spoken in many years, and the book

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.060
reveals what each of them has been up to since
they moved away from Derry.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.140
"I'll be forty and far away from
here.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.720
I thought you said you wanted to get out of
this town too."

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:03.440
Some of their professions remained the same
from the book to the film — Ben is an architect,

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.790
Beverly is a fashion designer, Bill is an
author and screenwriter, and Mike is the Derry

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:08.780
town librarian.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.160
But two of the Losers' professions were changed
for the film.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.100
The screen version of Richie Tozier is now
a stand-up comedian rather than a radio DJ,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:22.760
and instead of a limo driver, Chapter Two's
Eddie Kaspbrak is now a risk assessment analyst.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:27.300
In King's version of the story, none of the
characters were exactly happy about having

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.560
to fight IT again, but still felt bound by
the promise they made as children.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.599
In contrast, the adult characters in the movie
are all determined to leave Derry once they

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:40.909
figure out what's going on, leaving the town
at the mercy of IT, and need to be dragged

00:02:40.909 --> 00:02:43.220
back into the Losers Club kicking and screaming.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:48.920
We never learn exactly what IT is, in either
the book or the movies, but both attempt to

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.260
give at least a partial explanation.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.360
IT is some sort of ancient malevolent force
that crashed into the Earth millions of years

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.160
ago, in the spot that would one day become
Derry.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.400
In both versions of the story, characters
learn this backstory through a vision, but

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:07.010
the circumstances surrounding that vision
are very different in the films than in the

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:07.500
book.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:13.200
The way King wrote it, the child Losers attempt
to mimic a Native ritual that Ben read about

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.160
to try to induce visions that will help them
learn more about IT.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.120
Only Mike and Richie end up being successful,
and are temporarily transported millions of

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.500
years into the past, where they witness IT's
arrival on Earth.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.140
However, in the movie, it's the adult Mike
that gets the vision, through seeking out

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:34.980
a Native tribe who give him drugs that enable
him to see the arrival of IT, along with the

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.319
ritual their tribe used to fight IT.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.140
"Can you torch me?"

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.500
Another big reveal the book made about IT
is the fact that IT is female, and the version

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.820
of IT that the adult Losers encounter is pregnant.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.480
It's probably for the best that the films
left this part out, leaving IT as a genderless,

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.480
ageless entity.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:58.620
The film feels more self-contained and definitive,
without the sense that it might be teasing

00:03:58.620 --> 00:03:59.340
a sequel.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.580
In the book, after the Losers' reunion dinner
at the Asian restaurant, the six adults decide

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:08.780
to split up and move around Derry on their
own, allowing instinct to guide them.

00:04:09.089 --> 00:04:12.939
The characters in the book have a feeling
that in order to defeat IT, they'll need to

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:18.030
get back into the mindset they had as children,
casting aside their adult tendencies to make

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.180
plans, and following their guts instead.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.879
In the film, while the Losers still split
up, their motivation is completely reversed.

00:04:24.879 --> 00:04:29.430
Mike tasks each of them with recovering their
childhood memories through finding a "token,"

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.540
some sort of significant object that will
help empower them to defeat IT.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.440
Having such a defined plan seems to work against
the book's insistence that defeating IT will

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.600
require the adult Losers to embrace the uncertainty
and imagination of childhood.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.440
In a big departure from the novel, Richie
is revealed to have been harboring romantic

00:04:48.449 --> 00:04:50.940
feelings for Eddie ever since they were kids.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.240
In the book, the adult Richie is single, but
his sexuality never really comes into play.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.940
However, IT: Chapter Two spends much of the
film hinting that Richie is hiding his sexuality,

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.240
and Pennywise preys on his fear of someone
discovering the truth.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:08.280
Richie's big fear in the book is eyeballs,
which is definitely creepy, but doesn't have

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.220
much of an emotional weight to it.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.919
On the other hand, Richie's heartbreaking
reaction to Eddie's death at the end of Chapter

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:19.580
Two helps drive home just how high the cost
of the Losers' victory was.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.980
And at the end of the film, Richie's visit
to the fence where he carved his and Eddie's

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.620
initials as kids, and his smile as he traces
the letters with his fingers, hints that his

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.340
fear of discovery is now gone, killed with
Pennywise.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.740
In the book, while IT does assume the appearance
of Pennywise a fair amount of the time, it

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.640
also spends a lot of time in a number of other
forms.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:45.400
Some of these guises are included in the films,
but several others are either not included

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.060
or are only shown extremely briefly.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.759
In the book, IT appears to several of the
children as a gigantic bird, and during the

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:57.800
climactic battle in the tunnels, it turns
into a giant eyeball and then a massive spider.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.900
The films did include a version of the spider,
but cut way back on the rest, substituting

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:07.650
them with new, equally disturbing Pennywise
scenes, such as when the clown comes out of

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.800
the projector to the kids in Chapter One.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.289
The way IT plays with the Losers' perception
of reality was also altered for the films.

00:06:15.289 --> 00:06:19.849
In the book, during the scene when Beverly
goes to visit her childhood home, Mrs. Kersh's

00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:25.110
house transforms into sticky, sugary candy,
playing on Beverly's childhood fear of the

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.680
witch from Hansel and Gretel, while the film
just sees it change from the warm apartment

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.060
Beverly had perceived at first to the dilapidated
building it truly was.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.240
This is just one of the many changes the films
make to ITs many appearances, while still

00:06:39.249 --> 00:06:44.650
maintaining the terrifying sense that IT can
be anywhere, at any time, and we may never

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:46.240
know until it's too late.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.940
In the book, Henry Bowers attacks Mike at
the library, stabbing him in the femoral artery

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.180
and putting him in the hospital, before heading
to the hotel, where he's killed by Eddie.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:01.120
In the film, these events are reversed, with
Henry appearing in Eddie's room first.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:05.400
Henry then attacks Mike at the library, but
the other Losers are right behind him after

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.979
his attack on Eddie, and Henry is killed by
Richie before he can do any serious damage

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:10.540
to Mike.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.960
This sets up an extremely different dynamic
for the final showdown between the adult Losers

00:07:15.969 --> 00:07:20.050
and IT, since in the book, Mike is in the
hospital and the rest of the group is forced

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:24.160
to take IT on with only five people, one of
whom has a broken arm.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:29.240
In the film, all six are present and able-bodied
when they take on IT.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.140
IT is not the only ancient supernatural force
present in Stephen King's book.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.860
There's also an ancient, cosmic turtle, which
assists the child Losers in their quest to

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:39.860
defeat IT.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:44.699
The turtle exists in a sort of void outside
of our reality, where Bill interacts with

00:07:44.699 --> 00:07:49.110
it and it gives him the Ritual of Chüd, a
battle of wills that draws on the power of

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:50.900
Bill's childhood imagination.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:56.060
While the films contain a few subtle visual
nods to the turtle, that's as far as its involvement

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:56.560
goes.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.819
Instead, the Ritual of Chüd is a Native ritual
which involves the burning of the Losers'

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:05.820
tokens in a mysterious artifact, chanting,
and strong belief.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:10.840
It's eventually revealed that the ritual failed
the Native tribe who attempted to use it,

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.640
and that Mike knew the whole time.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.620
The ritual fails, and it is ultimately the
Losers coming together to make Pennywise small

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.240
in their minds that allows them to defeat
IT.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.219
The town of Derry is much more of a character
in the novel than in the films, and it is

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:30.939
strongly implied that the town itself is linked
to IT and sustained by the same ancient evil.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.839
So when the adult Losers go on their final
quest through the sewers to destroy IT, the

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.800
town gets increasingly more wounded.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.620
Derry gets pummeled by a vicious storm, and
the townspeople begin to awaken from IT's

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.880
thrall, feeling for the first time as though
something may be wrong.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:51.380
The closer the Losers get to their goal, the
more Derry suffers, with pipes exploding violently

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:56.260
and buildings collapsing, until eventually
all of downtown Derry is destroyed.

00:08:56.510 --> 00:09:01.020
The film keeps the destruction of Pennywise
much more contained, with only the house on

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.840
Neibolt Street collapsing in on itself after
the Losers succeed.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:08.860
Presumably the tunnels underneath the town
have also caved in, but it doesn't seem to

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.900
have any impact at all on the conditions on
the surface.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.340
One of the strange things about living in
Derry, in both the book and the films, is

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:21.190
that once you leave the town, your memory
of it slowly fades, until you don't even remember

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:22.800
that you ever lived there.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:27.500
A big part of the adult Losers' storylines
is the effort to recover their lost memories,

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:32.260
since until Mike called, none of them remembered
knowing each other, or that they'd ever fought

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:33.520
IT as children.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:38.000
In the book, this forgetting effect doesn't
change after the defeat of Pennywise.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:43.860
Mike ends the novel musing that this forgetting
must mean that IT is truly dead now, and wondering

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:48.600
how long it will be until even his journal
entries are completely erased.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.320
However, IT: Chapter Two takes the complete
opposite approach.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.680
Instead of bidding each other sad farewells,
knowing they'll never see or remember one

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:58.800
another again, they keep in touch.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.600
While the film's happy ending is not as bittersweet
as King's, it still serves to convey that

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.060
this time, the Losers were successful, and
Pennywise is indeed gone for good.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:14.060
The tragic death of Stan Uris by his own hand
hangs heavy over the characters in both the

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:18.210
book and IT: Chapter Two, with his friends
all assuming that he took his life because

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:19.160
he was afraid.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:24.639
However, the film gives Stan a last goodbye
that the book never does, in the form of identical

00:10:24.639 --> 00:10:29.450
letters he writes to each of the Losers, which
he leaves for his wife to mail at some point

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:30.240
following his death.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.560
He explains that he didn't take his life for
the reasons they probably thought.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:38.910
He says that he knew he couldn't bring himself
to return to Derry, but that he also knew

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:42.970
that the cosmic strength that the Losers held
when they were all together might not reach

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.460
its full potential if one of them didn't believe.

00:10:45.829 --> 00:10:50.540
Giving Stan some of the final words is an
effective way of communicating that, even

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:52.580
in death, Losers stick together.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:11:01.420
“But if we stick together, all of us, we'll
win."

